---
title: Onigiri (rice balls)
country: Japan
region: Osaka (Kansai)
credit: Home cooks and onigiri shops of Osaka; taught in-game by Haruka
cook: haruka
difficulty: 2
skills: [grains, heat-control, shaping]
servings: 6
license: CC-BY-SA-4.0
status: draft
---
-- Draft until reviewed by a cook from this tradition. 360 ml rice = 2 rice-cooker cups (about 300 g).
Measure @Japanese short-grain rice{360%ml} into a #bowl{}. Add cold water, swirl gently with your fingertips and pour it off quickly. Repeat three or four times, until the water is only slightly cloudy. Drain in a #sieve{}.

Put the rice in a #heavy pot with a lid{} with @water{400%ml} and let it soak. ~{30%minutes}

Bring to a boil over medium-high heat with the lid on, then turn the heat to its lowest and cook without lifting the lid. ~{13%minutes}

Turn off the heat and let the rice steam, lid still on. ~{10%minutes}

Meanwhile, make okaka: mix @katsuobushi{5%g} (bonito flakes) with @soy sauce{1%tsp}. Pit @umeboshi{2} and tear them into pieces.

Fluff the rice with a wet #rice paddle{} and let it cool until you can hold it comfortably — warm, not hot. ~{5%minutes}

Wet your hands and rub a pinch of @salt{1%tsp} over your palms. Take about 100 g of rice, press a dent in the middle, add a little filling, and close the rice over it.

Cup one hand into a V and turn the ball into a triangle with three or four gentle presses — firm enough to hold, never squashed. Wrap each in a strip of nori (@nori sheets{2}, cut in thirds) just before eating.
